1
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:09,977
The world.

2
00:00:10,060 --> 00:00:11,877
What a glorious place.

3
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:16,117
Seek freedom, and it will lie
stretched out before your eyes.

4
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:19,340
If the endless dream guides
your restless spirit,

5
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:23,960
seize it, raise your flag, and stand tall.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,717
I think back to the time
when my search first began.

7
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:35,297
I left behind those shores of sand.

8
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:38,700
For the endless everlasting
world out there.

9
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:42,817
I know that with this
burning passion driving me.

10
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:48,540
Someday, somewhere, I'll finally see the
light I have searched for everywhere?.

11
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:55,757
When the summer sun is shining.

12
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:58,537
Shaking the sails of my heart. I know.

13
00:00:58,620 --> 00:01:04,417
A brand new world

14
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:05,817
that has been hiding.

15
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:08,920
Has opened up its doors for me to go.

16
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:15,377
Wearing in the troubled waves.

17
00:01:15,460 --> 00:01:17,877
From all despair I can be saved.

18
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:21,177
For the horizon on the other side is.

19
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:28,597
And though I've traveled far
from where my search began.

20
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:31,197
Over seas of green and shores of sand.

21
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:34,560
Through the endless
everlasting world I bear.

22
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:38,697
I know that with this
burning passion driving me.

23
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:44,420
Some days I'm where I'll finally see
the light I will search for everywhere?

24
00:01:54,580 --> 00:01:57,400
I can't believe our luck.

25
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:00,960
Seriously, that orangutan almost sunk us.

26
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:04,897
And now it's up to me to fix it
all and keep this thing afloat.

27
00:02:04,980 --> 00:02:07,997
Seems to me that this busted
old ship is on our last leg.

28
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:09,077
We need a new one.

29
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:11,617
How dare you even consider
replacing this boat?

30
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:12,797
Yeah, how dare you?

31
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:14,337
We all know the story, Usopp.

32
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:16,244
And try not to be so defensive.

33
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:18,937
The going mare is an
important part of our crew,

34
00:02:19,020 --> 00:02:20,957
and we all know we have
to take care of her.

35
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:23,317
You shouldn't take
everything so personally.

36
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:24,230
You're right.

37
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:25,220
I'm sorry.

38
00:02:27,820 --> 00:02:28,650
Oops.

39
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:34,617
We left Mocktown and
headed east by circling

40
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:36,317
around the northern shore of the island.

41
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:38,300
Our destination is here.

42
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:40,137
We're supposed to meet that man there.

43
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:41,050
What's his name?

44
00:02:41,133 --> 00:02:42,517
His name is Mockblot Cricket.

45
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:45,953
And you're sure that he'll be
able to help us out, right?

46
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:48,280
Whoa! Amazing!

47
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:51,134
That's where he lives?

48
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:52,700
It's a freaking palace!

49
00:02:53,020 --> 00:02:55,077
I've never seen a house that big before!

50
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:58,101
This Mont Blanc cricket
guy must be really loaded!

51
00:02:58,680 --> 00:02:59,620
Look closer.

52
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:01,291
It's really unattractive.

53
00:03:01,540 --> 00:03:05,364
A guy's got to have some serious
guts to build a house that ugly.

54
00:03:05,740 --> 00:03:06,600
What's that?

55
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:14,000
This isn't a palace at all! What?

56
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:16,697
The actual house is only half that size.

57
00:03:16,780 --> 00:03:18,597
What you see is a facade, a fake.

58
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:20,857
This guy's even more vain than I thought.

59
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:24,497
Why exactly did Cricket decide to
leave his home and come live here?

60
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:26,286
I don't know all the details,

61
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:28,917
but I think it has something
to do with his belief.

62
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,837
that a large amount of gold rests
somewhere in the hills of Jaya.

63
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:32,750
Gold?

64
00:03:32,833 --> 00:03:34,837
Do you think he's here searching
for some sort of buried treasure?

65
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:37,140
Well, I just don't know.

66
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:38,830
Go!

67
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:41,200
Try over here, Chopper.

68
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:42,377
Take out this spot.

69
00:03:42,460 --> 00:03:43,989
I'll be right there, Nami.

70
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:46,937
Must be hard to live alone
in such a remote area.

71
00:03:47,020 --> 00:03:47,850
Hello!

72
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:49,569
Don't just barge in there!

73
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:51,825
Huh, looks like nobody's home.

74
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:53,000
Anybody here?

75
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:55,320
I guess he's gone.

76
00:03:55,620 --> 00:03:56,717
Get out of there, you idiot.

77
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:58,357
This guy could be dangerous.

78
00:03:58,440 --> 00:03:59,620
Hey, you guys.

79
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:00,880
Nobody's home.

80
00:04:01,880 --> 00:04:03,720
Huh, what's this?

81
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:06,830
A book?

82
00:04:06,913 --> 00:04:08,500
And it looks pretty old.

83
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:09,870
Huh.

84
00:04:09,953 --> 00:04:11,640
It's called Nolan the Liar.

85
00:04:12,660 --> 00:04:13,490
Huh.

86
00:04:13,573 --> 00:04:14,917
Sounds like it could be good.

87
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:16,294
I sure like the title.

88
00:04:16,840 --> 00:04:19,400
Nolan the Liar? Yeah.
Have you heard of it?

89
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:23,217
It says here it was originally
published in the North Blue Territory.

90
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:24,130
It was.

91
00:04:24,213 --> 00:04:25,977
I was born in the North Blue Territory.

92
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:28,217
I'm certain I must have
told you guys that.

93
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:29,130
No?

94
00:04:29,213 --> 00:04:30,043
That's news to me.

95
00:04:30,126 --> 00:04:31,357
I always thought you
were born in the East.

96
00:04:31,440 --> 00:04:32,277
I grew up there.

97
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:33,517
Enough already, Chopper.

98
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:34,718
What are you doing?

99
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:38,840
Where I come from, this is a famous story.

100
00:04:39,100 --> 00:04:42,397
It's a fairy tale supposedly
based on the life of Noland,

101
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:45,068
who lived in the North
Blue a long time ago.

102
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:50,560
This is a story of ancient origins.

103
00:04:50,940 --> 00:04:54,600
The events herein transpired
more than 400 years ago.

104
00:04:55,420 --> 00:04:58,820
There once was a man by the
name of Montblanc Noland,

105
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:01,373
who lived in the North Blue countryside.

106
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:03,600
Noland, who was an explorer,

107
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:07,837
always regaled the crowds with
stories of his incredible exploits,

108
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:11,979
but nobody knew whether or not to
take Noland's tall tales seriously.

109
00:05:12,580 --> 00:05:15,580
One day, upon returning
from yet another adventure,

110
00:05:15,820 --> 00:05:18,277
he reported to his king
all that he had seen.

111
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:22,540
I saw a city of gold on an island
in the Great Sea, declared Noland,

112
00:05:22,700 --> 00:05:27,406
and the courageous king decided that he
must have this treasure for his kingdom.

113
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:32,617
The king set out with Noland and 2,000
of his finest soldiers. Along the way,

114
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:36,837
they endured treacherous storms and
battled many ferocious sea creatures.

115
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:38,557
At the end of their journey,

116
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:40,020
only the king, Noland,

117
00:05:40,180 --> 00:05:44,797
and 100 soldiers survived to set foot on
the island that housed the City of Gold.

118
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:46,917
And all they found for
their troubles was a

119
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,706
normal island covered
with an ordinary jungle.

120
00:05:49,980 --> 00:05:51,500
And so upon their return,

121
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:55,297
Noland was sentenced to death for
the crime of lying to his king.

122
00:05:55,380 --> 00:05:59,397
His last words were as
follows I can only assume

123
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:02,337
that the city of gold
sunk into the sea the

124
00:06:02,420 --> 00:06:05,057
king and his court were
dumbfounded nobody believed

125
00:06:05,140 --> 00:06:07,537
in Oland anymore and the
last of his days were

126
00:06:07,620 --> 00:06:10,037
spent in shame and ridicule
before his sentence

127
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:12,517
of death was carried out
before a cheering crowd

128
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:15,337
it says here that the
book is based on the true

129
00:06:20,240 --> 00:06:24,240
Maud Blanc Cricket is an ascendant
of the infamous Maud Blanc Nolan!

130
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:28,077
I heard that fairy tale
all the time as a kid,

131
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:31,777
but I never knew that Jaya was the island
where he thought he saw the City of Gold.

132
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:35,857
When we were growing up in the North Blue,
we were always warned that if we lied,

133
00:06:35,940 --> 00:06:38,237
we'd suffer the same fate
as Nolan and be ridiculed?

134
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:40,137
until we were eventually put to death.

135
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:44,677
It makes perfect sense that
Cricket's related to that fool.

136
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:48,317
But I can't believe that Jaya is
the island from the fairy tale.

137
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:51,157
Pardon the intrusion, but
if I heard you correctly,

138
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:53,037
you're saying a descendant
of Nolan is here

139
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:55,857
400 years after the fact
looking for that gold?

140
00:06:55,940 --> 00:06:57,997
He's got to be doing
this to clear his family

141
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:00,727
name and regain some
small amount of respect.

142
00:07:05,220 --> 00:07:07,780
This story keeps getting
better and better.

143
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:12,537
Can he seriously hope to
wipe away over 400 years of

144
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:15,317
embarrassment by bringing
back a few pieces of gold?

145
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:16,230
What a joke!

146
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:20,586
Sounds like he's a few
bricks short of a wall!

147
00:07:29,940 --> 00:07:34,020
Alas, the liar died before he
could prove himself to the people.

148
00:07:35,760 --> 00:07:39,701
All he ever really wanted to do
was live the life of an adventurer.

149
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:41,877
That last part's not in the book!

150
00:07:41,960 --> 00:07:44,960
Don't try to tack on your
own sentimental nonsense.

151
00:07:45,820 --> 00:07:47,585
Did he just fall in the water?

152
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:49,200
What's going on?

153
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:51,540
Who the hell are you?

154
00:07:52,020 --> 00:07:55,257
You've got nerve relaxing at
someone's house without permission?

155
00:07:55,340 --> 00:07:57,577
This is my land and I suggest you leave!

156
00:07:57,660 --> 00:08:00,137
Hurry Usopp, move it, go and
fish Luffy out of the sea!

157
00:08:00,220 --> 00:08:02,960
I know why you're here,
you're after my goal.

158
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:16,993
Don't worry, this guy's a terrible shot.

159
00:08:18,760 --> 00:08:19,960
Hey, cut it out!

160
00:08:20,100 --> 00:08:22,240
Idiot, never underestimate your bow.

161
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:25,701
Out of the way, I'll handle this!

162
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:36,137
You really need to learn
to be more careful.

163
00:08:36,220 --> 00:08:39,420
But I thought I saw bubbles in the water.

164
00:08:40,260 --> 00:08:42,997
So I looked closer, and
then a chestnut appeared.

165
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:44,517
But it wasn't a chestnut.

166
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:46,600
It was a guy, and he pulled me in.

167
00:08:48,980 --> 00:08:49,921
What's with him?

168
00:08:52,180 --> 00:08:53,720
I need more cold towels.

169
00:08:54,040 --> 00:08:55,569
And open up those windows.

170
00:08:57,020 --> 00:08:58,373
What's caisson disease?

171
00:08:58,540 --> 00:09:00,157
So this guy is really sick, huh?

172
00:09:00,240 --> 00:09:03,937
Yeah, caisson disease is an illness
that sometimes affects divers.

173
00:09:04,020 --> 00:09:07,157
Though it's called a disease, it
isn't actually a chronic condition.

174
00:09:07,240 --> 00:09:11,017
It's caused by a lack of decompression
when surfacing from the seabed.

175
00:09:11,100 --> 00:09:13,817
The nitrogen in the blood
releases too quickly and

176
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:16,997
causes gas bubbles to form
in the circulatory system.

177
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:19,057
And when those gas bubbles expand,

178
00:09:19,140 --> 00:09:21,157
they can cause considerable
damage to muscles

179
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:23,837
and joints and seriously
decrease blood flow.

180
00:09:23,920 --> 00:09:25,557
So it's a mystery disease.

181
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:26,470
Think you can help him?

182
00:09:26,553 --> 00:09:27,540
I'm not sure.

183
00:09:27,780 --> 00:09:31,897
My guess is he kept diving every day without
allowing time for his body to recover,

184
00:09:31,980 --> 00:09:33,457
and it finally caught up with him.

185
00:09:33,540 --> 00:09:35,357
That's why he passed out during the fight.

186
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:37,357
But why in the world did he do that?

187
00:09:37,440 --> 00:09:40,240
I don't know, but he
was taking a big risk.

188
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:45,440
In some cases, caisson disease can
cause death, so he's lucky to be alive.

189
00:09:58,220 --> 00:10:01,397
Oh, hey, Mashida, I guess
you're still alive after all.

190
00:10:01,480 --> 00:10:03,680
Why do you say that? Forget about it.

191
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:05,917
Do you know what all that
gunfire was about earlier?

192
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:09,317
I'm not sure, but that's where
I'm headed now to find out.

193
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:10,257
Well, come on then.

194
00:10:10,340 --> 00:10:12,222
I'm worried about the old-timer.

195
00:10:13,980 --> 00:10:15,980
Hey, old-timer, are you all right?

196
00:10:18,260 --> 00:10:19,800
They've come to kill us!

197
00:10:20,420 --> 00:10:22,697
What in the world are
you people doing here?

198
00:10:22,780 --> 00:10:24,617
And just what are you
doing to our friends?

199
00:10:24,700 --> 00:10:25,917
We're trying to help,

200
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:29,037
so I suggest you either lend us a
hand or find someone else to buy it.

201
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:30,397
Don't antagonize them, Luffy!

202
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:31,577
You know what they're capable of!

203
00:10:31,660 --> 00:10:33,777
And now there's two of
those savages to deal with!

204
00:10:33,860 --> 00:10:34,801
They're savages!

205
00:10:34,920 --> 00:10:37,449
Everyone jump out the
windows and run away!

206
00:10:37,700 --> 00:10:38,800
Thank you, guys!

207
00:10:40,360 --> 00:10:41,419
What did they say?

208
00:10:42,440 --> 00:10:44,616
So you guys live on this island, too?

209
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:45,830
Yep,

210
00:10:45,913 --> 00:10:47,177
this old-timer's house is the official

211
00:10:47,260 --> 00:10:49,966
headquarters of the
Saruyama Alliance of Jaya.

212
00:10:50,080 --> 00:10:52,777
We call this home, but we spend
most of our time on our ships!

213
00:10:52,860 --> 00:10:55,957
We're more comfortable at sea, because
this place is too small for us.

214
00:10:56,040 --> 00:10:58,117
You guys are too big for your own good.

215
00:10:58,200 --> 00:10:59,897
But it's all in how you
look at it, I guess.

216
00:10:59,980 --> 00:11:03,637
To a giant, you'd still look like
nothing more than a ball of earwax.

217
00:11:03,720 --> 00:11:06,497
Well, those three sure are getting
along well now, aren't they?

218
00:11:06,580 --> 00:11:08,677
Yeah, seems like they
have a lot in common.

219
00:11:08,760 --> 00:11:11,417
I'm pretty impressed you managed
to get Machida here off your ship.

220
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:14,377
It's quite a feat for someone as
small as you to overpower him.

221
00:11:14,460 --> 00:11:16,257
Eh, it wasn't really that hard.

222
00:11:16,340 --> 00:11:17,170
What's that?

223
00:11:17,253 --> 00:11:18,922
I'm sure you could kick his ass.

224
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:22,040
Wolf, he's waking up. He's awake?

225
00:11:22,860 --> 00:11:23,690
Yeah!

226
00:11:25,660 --> 00:11:26,490
He's right!

227
00:11:26,680 --> 00:11:28,420
I can't kick Masi's ass!

228
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:33,100
Hey, chestnut head, I
got a question for you.

229
00:11:37,540 --> 00:11:39,060
I'm sorry I attacked you.

230
00:11:39,280 --> 00:11:40,937
I thought you were
nothing more than another

231
00:11:41,020 --> 00:11:43,317
group of bandits who had
come to steal my gold.

232
00:11:43,400 --> 00:11:45,237
Did someone say something about gold?

233
00:11:45,320 --> 00:11:46,477
Just calm down, Nami.

234
00:11:46,560 --> 00:11:48,797
What exactly was it you wanted to ask me?

235
00:11:48,880 --> 00:11:50,397
We're searching for Sky Island,

236
00:11:50,480 --> 00:11:52,777
and we were told you might be
able to tell us how to get there.

237
00:11:52,860 --> 00:11:54,380
Are you serious?

238
00:11:58,680 --> 00:12:01,680
Don't tell me you really
believe that place exists!

239
00:12:02,360 --> 00:12:03,200
Stop it!

240
00:12:03,340 --> 00:12:04,637
You can't attack a sick man!

241
00:12:04,720 --> 00:12:06,117
You mean there's no Sky Island?

242
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:07,040
Are you sure?

243
00:12:10,160 --> 00:12:11,140
I don't know.

244
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:15,840
See, nobody knows if the Sky
Island really exists or not.

245
00:12:16,280 --> 00:12:19,997
I know of only one man who claims
to have seen the island for himself,

246
00:12:20,080 --> 00:12:22,845
but he's considered a
liar of epic proportions.

247
00:12:23,900 --> 00:12:25,312
What are you looking at?

248
00:12:26,440 --> 00:12:30,357
There's an old fairy tale that the
people of the North Blue know very well.

249
00:12:30,440 --> 00:12:32,300
It's called Nolan the Liar.

250
00:12:33,760 --> 00:12:35,237
Stop looking at me, Luffy!

251
00:12:35,320 --> 00:12:37,320
My name's not Nolan! Is he right?

252
00:12:37,520 --> 00:12:40,755
And is this island the one
mentioned in the fairy tale?

253
00:12:41,160 --> 00:12:44,600
Yeah, Nolan's my grandfather's
grandfather's grandfather.

254
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,882
He's a distant ancestor of mine.

255
00:12:46,980 --> 00:12:48,360
I am his descendant.

256
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:50,031
But we're so far removed,

257
00:12:50,120 --> 00:12:53,649
I doubt there's even a single
drop of his blood in my veins.

258
00:12:53,800 --> 00:12:57,857
The Mont Blanc family was driven out of
the North Blue Country a long time ago,

259
00:12:57,940 --> 00:13:01,234
and we've managed to keep a
very low profile ever since.

260
00:13:01,580 --> 00:13:05,717
People still denounce the family name,
but we've never despised him for this!

261
00:13:05,800 --> 00:13:07,040
But why?

262
00:13:07,460 --> 00:13:10,977
Because Nolan, contrary to popular
belief, was a very honest person.

263
00:13:11,060 --> 00:13:13,037
That's not what the fairy tale said.

264
00:13:13,120 --> 00:13:17,120
As the story goes, Nolan's
last words were as follows.

265
00:13:17,900 --> 00:13:21,137
I can only assume that the
City of Gold sunk into the sea.

266
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:24,985
This line is accompanied by an
idiotic face in the illustration.

267
00:13:25,340 --> 00:13:27,300
But Noland was no idiot.

268
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:31,117
He knew what he saw, and I
believe he wasn't lying about it.

269
00:13:31,200 --> 00:13:33,937
Noland was sure the island
he stumbled upon where he

270
00:13:34,020 --> 00:13:36,997
found the ruins of a city of
gold was the island of Jaya.

271
00:13:37,080 --> 00:13:40,256
But when he returned with
the king, there was no gold.

272
00:13:41,280 --> 00:13:42,110
Unsuccessful,

273
00:13:42,193 --> 00:13:45,777
he insisted that the ancient city had
submerged due to shifts in the Earth's crust.

274
00:13:45,860 --> 00:13:49,919
But everyone dismissed this as the
last-ditch excuse of a doomed man.

275
00:13:50,680 --> 00:13:52,798
Noland was given a public execution.

276
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:55,296
When he died, so did his discoveries.

277
00:13:55,580 --> 00:13:57,417
Only the legend of the Liar remained.

278
00:13:57,500 --> 00:13:58,330
I get it!

279
00:13:58,413 --> 00:14:00,737
You're trying to find the Ancient City
of Gold at the bottom of the sea in

280
00:14:00,820 --> 00:14:03,697
order to finally clear the Mont Blanc
family name and prove Noland right!

281
00:14:03,780 --> 00:14:04,956
Don't be ridiculous!

282
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:12,157
I don't really care if the Ancient
City of Gold exists or not.

283
00:14:12,240 --> 00:14:15,637
The actions of my ancestor have
nothing to do with me and my life.

284
00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:18,077
You can't possibly begin
to imagine what it's like?

285
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:20,517
As a kid for everyone to
laugh at you and ridicule

286
00:14:20,600 --> 00:14:23,306
you because you're a
descendant of that idiot.

287
00:14:25,120 --> 00:14:25,950
Noland.

288
00:14:26,033 --> 00:14:27,757
That's what I've dealt with my whole life.

289
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:28,670
Anyway,

290
00:14:28,753 --> 00:14:30,560
over the past 400 years,

291
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:34,517
a great many of my family have
set sail to find the city of gold,

292
00:14:34,600 --> 00:14:37,700
prove Noland right, and
restore the Mont Blanc name.

293
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:40,297
But each and every one
of them went missing.

294
00:14:40,380 --> 00:14:43,457
I was ashamed to be a member
of such a pathetic family.

295
00:14:43,540 --> 00:14:47,220
So I decided to leave home
and become a pirate. Wow!

296
00:14:47,480 --> 00:14:49,137
So you're a pirate just like us?

297
00:14:49,220 --> 00:14:51,477
I didn't even really
want to become a pirate.

298
00:14:51,560 --> 00:14:54,900
I just wanted out of Nolan's
shadow to be my own man.

299
00:14:55,300 --> 00:14:58,740
But over time, I came to
assemble my own pirate crew.

300
00:14:58,980 --> 00:15:00,862
Finally, I was seen as a leader,

301
00:15:01,540 --> 00:15:05,197
not a laughingstock, and I spanned
the world looking for adventures.

302
00:15:05,280 --> 00:15:08,751
Then 10 years ago, So as my
crew and I sailed the calm sea,

303
00:15:08,940 --> 00:15:11,469
we discovered this
island purely by chance!

304
00:15:11,820 --> 00:15:12,800
And ironically,

305
00:15:13,120 --> 00:15:17,677
of all my family members who sought out
this place, who lost their lives doing so,

306
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:19,240
it was I who found it!

307
00:15:19,420 --> 00:15:21,820
I who despised the Mont Blanc name!

308
00:15:22,360 --> 00:15:25,595
We came ashore, but found no
trace of the City of Gold.

309
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:29,957
I stood on a cape of the island and
felt I was destined to be there!

310
00:15:30,040 --> 00:15:33,020
Let's settle this,
Noland, once and for all!

311
00:15:33,860 --> 00:15:34,690
You're on your own!

312
00:15:34,773 --> 00:15:36,517
We don't want anything to do with this.

313
00:15:36,600 --> 00:15:38,137
We're setting sail and leaving.

314
00:15:38,220 --> 00:15:40,777
If you want to stay here and
search for gold that doesn't exist,

315
00:15:40,860 --> 00:15:42,154
you're doing it alone.

316
00:15:43,520 --> 00:15:44,660
So long, Captain.

317
00:15:50,320 --> 00:15:52,660
I don't care if I find the city or not.

318
00:15:53,040 --> 00:15:55,569
I'm not here to prove
Nolan right or wrong.

319
00:15:55,660 --> 00:15:58,280
I have no interest in rewriting history.

320
00:15:58,940 --> 00:16:02,234
All I'm doing is following
what I believe is my destiny.

321
00:16:02,380 --> 00:16:04,280
This is between me and Nolan.

322
00:16:05,680 --> 00:16:11,240
I'm facing what I fear, and whatever the
outcome, the act is all that matters.

323
00:16:13,800 --> 00:16:16,040
Wow, what a sad story.

324
00:16:17,420 --> 00:16:19,008
But what about the monkeys?

325
00:16:19,120 --> 00:16:22,117
How did they come to be on
this island with you anyway?

326
00:16:22,200 --> 00:16:26,277
I'm sure that's another story of
bravery in the face of incredible odds.

327
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:28,957
Your life reads like a great
book, you know, Cricket?

328
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:31,057
They were just fans of the fairy tale.

329
00:16:31,140 --> 00:16:31,970
They're fans?

330
00:16:32,500 --> 00:16:34,120
How did they find you?

331
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:36,577
I think it was five or six years ago.

332
00:16:36,660 --> 00:16:39,131
They heard a rumor and set out to find me.

333
00:16:39,400 --> 00:16:42,757
Those two truly believe that the
City of Gold exists somewhere,

334
00:16:42,840 --> 00:16:44,664
and they're helping me find it.

335
00:16:47,320 --> 00:16:50,237
Shoujo, you have no idea what
you just got yourself into!

336
00:16:50,320 --> 00:16:51,457
Bring it on, you big ape!

337
00:16:51,540 --> 00:16:52,677
I'm ready when you are!

338
00:16:52,760 --> 00:16:54,537
Just step up and you'll get knocked down!

339
00:16:54,620 --> 00:16:58,740
The sea surrounding this island
is very deep, and in those dark,

340
00:16:58,920 --> 00:17:02,626
cold depths, you're surrounded
by a profound sense of solitude.

341
00:17:03,820 --> 00:17:08,080
When I first arrived here, I spent
my time searching the seabed alone.

342
00:17:08,920 --> 00:17:11,297
They entered my life
without permission and

343
00:17:11,380 --> 00:17:13,577
assigned themselves as
my crew without asking!

344
00:17:13,660 --> 00:17:16,297
And they do nothing
but fight all the time.

345
00:17:16,380 --> 00:17:19,380
But having these idiots
around puts me at ease.

346
00:17:20,040 --> 00:17:22,380
And truthfully, I enjoy their company?

347
00:17:23,240 --> 00:17:24,360
You understand?

348
00:17:24,780 --> 00:17:26,557
I understand completely.

349
00:17:26,640 --> 00:17:31,117
True friends lift each other up and give
each other strength in times of need!

350
00:17:31,200 --> 00:17:34,137
Are you just about finished
telling us your life story?

351
00:17:34,220 --> 00:17:36,396
Shut your mouth, you ungrateful brat!

352
00:17:38,420 --> 00:17:39,250
Stop it!

353
00:17:39,333 --> 00:17:42,379
Look, all I really want to
know about is Sky Island!

354
00:17:44,260 --> 00:17:46,084
You're a pushy guy, aren't you?

355
00:17:46,300 --> 00:17:47,947
But like I told you earlier,

356
00:17:48,080 --> 00:17:51,077
the only person who's ever
seen the Sky Island is Noland,

357
00:17:51,160 --> 00:17:55,337
and nobody knows if he was telling the
truth or if it was just another tall tale.

358
00:17:55,420 --> 00:17:57,937
Is there any record of
his trip to the island?

359
00:17:58,020 --> 00:18:00,785
Officially, no, but he
does mention it in here.

360
00:18:01,200 --> 00:18:02,237
What do you have there?

361
00:18:02,320 --> 00:18:04,320
That isn't Nolan's logbook, is it?

362
00:18:05,380 --> 00:18:06,210
Yeah, it is.

363
00:18:06,293 --> 00:18:08,260
Read from the bottom of that page.

364
00:18:09,040 --> 00:18:09,870
Amazing!

365
00:18:09,953 --> 00:18:12,360
This logbook is over 400 years old.

366
00:18:12,560 --> 00:18:16,960
June City, year 1120, a sunny
day on the Sea of Ephemeris.

367
00:18:17,140 --> 00:18:19,017
Upon departing from the Jolly Villa,

368
00:18:19,100 --> 00:18:21,917
we left the harbor and sailed
into the rising sun at a

369
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,235
heading of east by northeast
with the wind at our back.

370
00:18:25,740 --> 00:18:29,437
At midday, we happened to cross
a bumboat carrying a rare item.

371
00:18:29,520 --> 00:18:34,057
A single-person contraption
called a sky waver. This small,

372
00:18:34,140 --> 00:18:36,377
mysterious vessel can
generate its own wind

373
00:18:36,460 --> 00:18:38,897
and sail at great speeds
even on a still day.

374
00:18:38,980 --> 00:18:41,337
But the ski-like ship was
not easily maneuvered.

375
00:18:41,420 --> 00:18:44,957
I couldn't control it, although my
crew has taken quite a liking to it.

376
00:18:45,040 --> 00:18:46,757
I gotta get one of those things!

377
00:18:46,840 --> 00:18:47,670
Keep going!

378
00:18:47,753 --> 00:18:48,598
Don't stop reading!

379
00:18:49,020 --> 00:18:49,850
Uh,

380
00:18:49,933 --> 00:18:52,517
we were told the vessel had been
manufactured on the Sky Island

381
00:18:52,600 --> 00:18:55,297
and that many more interesting
devices could be found there.

382
00:18:55,380 --> 00:18:57,517
It brought to mind an
explorer friend of mine

383
00:18:57,600 --> 00:18:59,677
who once showed me the
most unusual specimen,

384
00:18:59,760 --> 00:19:00,780
a living skyfish.

385
00:19:01,240 --> 00:19:05,475
I have never seen a fish with such a
bizarre appearance in all my years!

386
00:19:05,560 --> 00:19:09,617
This island remains the only unexplored
domain and as a sailor and adventurer.

387
00:19:09,700 --> 00:19:12,757
I feel it is my destiny to one
day reach the sea in the sky.

388
00:19:12,840 --> 00:19:14,240
Mont Blanc Noland.

389
00:19:14,440 --> 00:19:15,897
Do you know what this means?

390
00:19:15,980 --> 00:19:17,921
The sky island must really exist!

391
00:19:18,040 --> 00:19:19,840
Just like Robin said it did!

392
00:19:20,040 --> 00:19:22,805
Now we just got to figure
out how to get there!

393
00:19:27,200 --> 00:19:28,030
You're up.

394
00:19:28,113 --> 00:19:28,943
You feel it?

395
00:19:29,026 --> 00:19:29,856
All right?

396
00:19:29,939 --> 00:19:30,769
Better than ever?

397
00:19:30,852 --> 00:19:31,837
Glad to hear it.

398
00:19:31,920 --> 00:19:35,057
Shut up.

399
00:19:35,140 --> 00:19:36,257
I need to ask you something.

400
00:19:36,340 --> 00:19:37,837
What do you think of those guys?

401
00:19:37,920 --> 00:19:39,377
Why ask an old timer?

402
00:19:39,460 --> 00:19:42,577
It seems that they're
determined to get to Sky Island.

403
00:19:42,660 --> 00:19:43,490
No matter what.

404
00:19:43,573 --> 00:19:45,357
Sky Island.

405
00:19:45,440 --> 00:19:46,437
You know?

406
00:19:46,520 --> 00:19:48,397
There's only one way
for them to get there.

407
00:19:48,480 --> 00:19:51,960
And if they try to get there
on their own, they'll die.

408
00:19:52,700 --> 00:19:53,530
Exactly.

409
00:19:53,960 --> 00:19:55,957
I think we should help them along.

410
00:19:56,040 --> 00:19:58,216
So what do you guys think about that?
